WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.340
You lose more money holding other altcoins or shitcoins or trying to follow the trends.

2
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:09.140
Life-changing type of wealth creation.

3
00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:16.520
I think that this is something that will be huge in the future because it's very easy for the mainstream investor to understand it.

4
00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:19.240
But you have to jump when it's started.

5
00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:25.440
Hello, it's Bonnie. In this episode, we'll be talking to one of the most interesting people I've ever met in crypto.

6
00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:31.300
He's from Spain. He gave out a lot of bitcoins on the street to random people when it was dirt cheap.

7
00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:33.860
So that's how long he's been in crypto.

8
00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:41.380
He's worked at Binance, FTX, and now at CoinCall, a platform that focuses on crypto options trading.

9
00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:43.080
You might be wondering why.

10
00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:48.540
Maybe because Bloomberg says options trading is the hottest thing right now on Wall Street.

11
00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:53.520
Since he has seen all the bear and bull markets, I'm going to ask him to predict the next bull run.

12
00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:55.040
Let's welcome Cheviar.

13
00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.500
Hi.

14
00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:20.540
Let's talk about how long you've been in crypto and what's the journey like?

15
00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:24.500
Well, I was lucky to start early, actually, because I was still at the university.

16
00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:27.080
It was in 2012 when I first knew about Bitcoin.

17
00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:31.220
And I was reading articles about finance because I graduated in economics.

18
00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:36.100
So I came across an article that was talking about a euro for Africa.

19
00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:38.180
So the same that we have in Europe, but for Africa.

20
00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:46.980
And they were talking about a digital currency that was going to be used among the population there using their mobile phones because almost no one had a bank account.

21
00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:48.880
And I was very interested about this.

22
00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:50.180
And this coin was called Bitcoin.

23
00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:58.080
After I graduated a few years later, I started working or writing for Cointelegraph, first in Spanish and then in English.

24
00:01:58.080 --> 00:01:59.760
I had a blog about economics.

25
00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:04.080
So a few years later, I also joined Binance in 2019.

26
00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:07.420
I was one of the early employees, one of the first 300.

27
00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:09.880
Now I believe they have more than 8,000 people.

28
00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:10.580
It's huge.

29
00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:12.340
It's a different company, I would say.

30
00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:14.720
But back then, it was more like a startup.

31
00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:21.000
And I was at Binance until 2022 when I decided to join FTX.

32
00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:23.180
I left Binance because I spent there three years.

33
00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:25.420
I thought it was the right time to move on.

34
00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:30.520
During my last weeks, I got an offer from a former colleague from Binance that was working at FTX.

35
00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:34.360
And they told me that they were opening the market in Europe, that they were looking for people.

36
00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:36.040
So I decided to jump.

37
00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:39.500
Back then, it was a good decision, I must say.

38
00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:40.860
I don't regret it.

39
00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:42.960
Nobody could know what was going to happen.

40
00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:46.500
But I enjoyed the short trip at FTX.

41
00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:49.480
After that, like everyone, I also lost money.

42
00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:50.540
My family lost money.

43
00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:52.240
So I decided to go on my own.

44
00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:57.820
Like more or less one month later, after the FTX crash, I got a message from a colleague.

45
00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:02.980
And he told me that there was a company called CoinCall that was very interesting,

46
00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:05.580
that I should talk to the CEO and have a call.

47
00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:07.480
So I had a call with the CEO.

48
00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:11.540
He convinced me because there's something that FTX missed.

49
00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:13.260
It was the custody of users' funds.

50
00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:16.200
CoinCall doesn't have the custody of users' funds.

51
00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:18.260
And that was very important for me after what happened.

52
00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:21.820
At least here, we cannot repeat what happened at FTX.

53
00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:25.220
We don't need to do any proof of reserve because we don't have the custody.

54
00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:29.500
And what CoinCall uses is a company, external company called Kobo,

55
00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:32.180
that is specialized in the custody of users' funds.

56
00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:33.460
It has insurance.

57
00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:35.360
So it's much better.

58
00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:36.420
And I felt more confident.

59
00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:43.220
What's the difference between these three exchanges?

60
00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:45.300
Because I feel like they're so different.

61
00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:49.780
Binance, as I was there for three years, the first early stage when I joined,

62
00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:51.180
it was a pure startup.

63
00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:55.960
Binance didn't have anything like only spot, no margin, no futures, nothing.

64
00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:59.800
